WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Kiwi Craze, a bird-brained adventure.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.420
Wally's got himself a kiwi cookbook, and your friends are the main ingredient.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:32.640
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.680
I'm Mike.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:34.820
And I'm Sean.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.940
Sean, forget about having a kiwi craze.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.740
I've got a kiwi crave.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:43.620
For kiwis?

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.240
For kiwi, yeah, the fruit.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.820
The fruit, not the bird.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.260
I don't know why, but when I see kiwi, I immediately think of the fruit, never the bird.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.260
No, same. Never the people of New Zealand either.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.320
Yeah, no. Oh, that's true.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.440
They are called that.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.100
There's too many things called kiwi.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.800
They're called that because that's like the national bird is a kiwi, and then that happened

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.400
afterwards. Like, is that the timeline?

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.320
Well, you're my fellow eagle, so I get that.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.760
Right, yeah, I guess that's how it goes, right?

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.160
What's up? What's up?

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.720
What's up, eagle? Oh, that's why he was named Soaring Eagle last week.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.820
We're just closing that loop.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.220
If you have no idea what I'm talking about, that means you didn't listen to last week's

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.200
episode, and therefore you should know that you have to listen to...

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.680
You can't... This week's episode won't make any sense if you don't listen to last week's,

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.520
guys. You can't just jump in on episode like 453.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.200
Oh man, imagine if that's the episode.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.200
I never check anymore what that number is.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.920
But you can't just jump into episode 453 if

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.680
you didn't listen to the first 452, guys.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.340
It's not how this works.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.160
We started serializing, I think, back in 2019.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.080
So you got a lot of catching up to do.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.840
Exactly. My computer froze for a second, so that's a good start.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.080
Cool. But yeah, the notes...

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.260
Yeah, I need the notes.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.960
Sorry, guys. I hate to tell you.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.380
It's not a script, though, at least.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.020
It used to be a script, but I do need the notes to have a show here.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.800
And, you know, the kiwi that we're talking about is not a fruit.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.000
Much as I would have liked to have played as some kind of fruit mascot, I'm sure that's coming, right?

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.280
Don't we get the California raisins?

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.520
Yeah, sure. But I meant like, I guess the raisins

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.640
aren't really a fruit anyway.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.020
They're dried up grapes.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.040
Yeah. I mean, it's still like at its core fruit.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.420
Yeah, but it's like... How's the computer doing?

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:31.780
It's altered. I'm good.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.680
I'm back now. So thank you so much, Sean.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:41.940
A kiwi, of course, being the flightless bird that you play as in this game, this flightless

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:49.320
bird has to steal balloons and rescue other kiwis.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.280
Yes. I just want to get to the heart of this.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.180
I was under the impression that this game was

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:07.740
called the New Zealand story, which one, very funny to just have a game just called the story

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.420
of a nation state.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.720
I don't know why that's funny.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.180
It's actually pretty normal, I feel like.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:18.840
Were you worried it was going to be like a Koei game where it's like a grand strategy New Zealand

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.080
game? I kind of did.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.600
I have no idea what that would entail.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.160
I don't really have a very good grasp

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:34.700
of New Zealander history, but I was surprised when it was just sort of like a puzzle platformer.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:43.100
And even beyond that, though, there's just a lot of surprising elements of this game that just feels

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:43.960
weird.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.560
It's a weird game, right?

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:51.980
I wasn't expecting a I wouldn't even call this a puzzle game, but like I wasn't expecting this

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:59.660
like super short repeatable format for a game called the New Zealand story or Kiwi craze.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.320
And it's called the New Zealand story literally everywhere else.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.840
It's just called Kiwi craze,

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.280
a bird brained adventure in North America.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.160
So it was only Nintendo of America that was like, this is kind of a nonsensical.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.160
Yeah, you guys got to make this more playful.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.100
And and, you know, to be to be fair, I don't really I'm

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:27.860
not a huge fan of just putting K on crazy and calling it a day like, oh, my God, that's so crazy

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.980
that they did that.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.180
They they added a K.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.820
But I I do like a bird brained adventure.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:35.940
I know you did.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.800
Yeah, you always love to see it like that.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.520
Yeah, I like I like a good subtitle and that's one of the best.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:47.460
Yeah. But if you wouldn't call this a puzzle platformer, what would you call it?

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:48.900
No, I have no idea.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:53.160
I just didn't want to call it a puzzle because it's not hard to solve.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.640
No, no, it's just weird.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.500
Yeah, exactly. You are.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:00.720
Apparently, this bird has a name.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.600
His name is Tiki, which, you know, is strange.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.000
Like Tiki, like that's Hawaiian.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:06.860
It's called a kiwi.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.120
Yeah. So he's a kiwi bird.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:17.600
And then, as I mentioned on the back of the box, this guy, Wally, he's a walrus and he

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.060
has made off with all of Tiki's friends.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:25.120
And apparently, according to the manual, it's selling them to other zoos.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.200
Other zoos being other zoos, though, being the concerning part.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.100
Like, so they were never free?

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.760
No, no, no. Like, they were always in zoos?

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.560
Yes. Huh.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:40.940
They just have a lot of national pride that they're in the New Zealand Zoo.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:43.540
Yeah, I can't trust the zoos ever since Harambe.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.560
Absolutely not.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.220
Yeah. And then Tiki, I guess, is like, you know, he's the one who got away.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:58.240
So he's got to be the one to also get them all back one cage at a time.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.180
And, you know, the interesting thing there is that, of course, the other zoos aren't, like,

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.720
next door. They are all throughout New Zealand.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.880
So the reason, I guess, why it was ever a New Zealand story is that after you complete

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:18.720
a stage, you find out the next, like, you know, city, town, whatever that you're visiting

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.100
in New Zealand where it's the next zoo.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:26.660
But don't worry, all the zoos look the same, and they're devoid of people or other animals.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.680
Or really color.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:30.060
Right. Color.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:40.120
Yeah, sure. I'm surprised how, like, a game that is this playful in vibe is so monochromatic visually.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:45.200
Like, yes, there are moments when you get to see, like, the sky and you get to see,

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.700
like, foliage and water.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:58.120
However, but I'd say, like, half of the visuals of this game are just shades of yellow.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.300
Not really sure.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.040
It's not even like it's the yellow of sand or whatever.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:08.940
It's just weird texture patterns of yellow.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.160
Yeah, it's a lot of yellow.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:13.280
And keep in mind, Tiki is yellow, too.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:21.140
And also, like, I don't love how the levels are laid out because they're both horizontal

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:26.460
and vertical, but they're also, like, walled off in a way where a lot of times it is actually

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.740
just one giant square that you have to, like, maneuver around.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.740
It's a maze with gravity.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:32.340
Right, exactly.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.420
And, like, that's not a great feeling.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.200
I get that they're trying to be like, oh, you see this?

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.020
Like, you'll get to this part eventually.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.360
But, like, that's kind of frustrating in a short stage.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.580
Like, just if I can see it now, why do I need to go around it?

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:55.360
That's whatever time, like, you know, when I opened up the newspaper when I was a kid

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.720
and there were mazes or maybe not a newspaper, but, like, a child's magazine.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:05.880
I was always like, what's the point of going through when I just draw the line, like, straight

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:06.800
through all the walls?

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.380
Yeah, exactly.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:09.640
What's the point? You just look.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:10.580
Why even draw anything?

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.720
Just look through the maze.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.520
Like, solved it. Yep.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:21.460
Done. Now, there are weird, I don't know what they, like, I don't know, sheep's heads that you

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:23.220
can sort of float on.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.500
I called them balloons.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.760
I felt stupid saying that, but I think that's, like, that's what they're doing, right?

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.000
It's, like, maybe those are the kinds of balloons they're giving out at that zoo.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.140
Oh, these are just New Zealander balloons.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:36.300
This is just what they do there.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.080
Yeah, yeah, yeah. They all have sheephead balloons.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.520
They actually look like cows to me.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:46.500
Maybe cow. I'm still going to go with sheep, but, yeah, I don't know.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.660
It's just a weird, like, it's a weird vehicle of choice anyway.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.280
Always. Like, why have...

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.480
Half the enemies are on them.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:59.680
But also, like, why not make the gimmick, if he's a flightless bird, that he can't fly?

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:01.180
Like, fly another way. Well, that is the thing.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.960
Like, he needs this sheep to fly.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:07.600
Right, but, like, no, but, like, the whole thing then being designed around flight is,

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.140
like, stupid. It's, like, instead, just make it a fun platformer of trying to figure out how to get there.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:18.040
Like, the fact that you can't just fly should be a point of the game, but instead it's

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:22.120
just like, oh, no, but he can fly, like, 90% of the time as long as you have this power-up.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.720
Yeah. I'll grant you that.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:30.220
I just think, like, you know, they're going for the fact of...

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:34.200
Because a flightless bird is quite a comedic thing, right?

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.820
Penguins. Penguins are really funny when they march.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:38.320
Penguins are funny. Emus are funny.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:40.900
Name a single flightless bird that isn't funny.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:42.980
I challenge you.

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:49.220
Yes. And inside, when you go to rescue these birds that are in the cages, they all, like, give

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.860
me Tweety Bird vibes right away.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.400
Oh, yeah. Yellow bird in a cage.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:55.460
Tweety Bird. Yep.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:01.600
So, where's, like, where are the enemies that want to eat these birds?

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.680
Where are they in this stage?

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:04.980
I'm going to be honest.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:10.720
I don't really know how to even talk about this game because it is just, it's just so

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:18.100
strange. It's kind of, like, how I imagine someone that has never designed a game might design

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:27.780
a game. I don't know if you have a lot of developer history or research done, but I do know that

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:32.800
this game was inspired by a trip one of the developers took.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:34.540
Yes. That's all you need to know.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:41.760
They captured New Zealand so well, or they went on a company all-expense-paid trip to New

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.880
Zealand and were like, shit, now I have to make a game.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.700
You phoned it in. Yeah, exactly.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:46.940
Like, what do I even do?

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.020
Oh, Kiwis.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.580
There we go. Stories like this, I can buy.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.900
Like, you know, when you hear that story about, like, oh, Zelda is based on, like, oh, when

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.260
I was a kid, I used to go on these adventures in the woods.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:00.880
And, like, I buy that.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:04.120
I get it. Like, it feels like an adventure in the woods, kind of.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:12.560
I don't get this as, like, I did a road trip of New Zealand, aside from maybe the map is

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.260
the route he took.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.760
Yeah, right? Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.440
Maybe this is the, all the different zoos that you go to are his trip.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.900
Or maybe he just went to a lot of zoos.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.300
He was like, yeah, there's got to be something I can do around zoos in a video game.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:31.000
But, you know, it's worth mentioning, this started on the arcade in 1988, and it was developed

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.480
by Taito, so, like, you know, very competent developer, made great arcade games.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.380
Maybe this is fun in, like, a short spurt, put 25 cents in and see how many stages you

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:50.900
can complete. But on the NES version of this game, you know, the bosses are probably the most interesting

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:52.440
thing about the game.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.940
And I was very surprised by the first boss.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.900
Yes. Because you have to be swallowed by it in order to even start fighting it.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.680
Okay. And I love that.

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:07.760
I think that that was like, wow, that made me, that was the first time in this puzzle

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:08.600
game that I thought.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:14.620
I think that the, to just know context, you have to be swallowed by it before anything is

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:24.180
very funny. For a little context, you fight a whale, and it kind of throws a bunch of projectiles at

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.800
you, kind of goes everywhere, and then he sweeps in, he swoops in and eats you.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:33.000
And I thought that that was like, you lost a life, you got to start over.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.580
And then you just kind of spawn inside the whale.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:42.560
And there's, I don't know, there's drips that, for a second, I also thought like, maybe

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.780
this is just its way of prolonging your death.

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:49.360
Because there's so many drip drops that it's kind of...

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.420
Drip drops look like water, but it should be like acid, right?

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:53.540
Like, from his stomach?

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:58.080
Yeah, maybe it should have been more, like, obviously something that's digesting you.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:07.280
But then you just start shooting, and the big blown up whale that you are inside of, it starts

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:08.960
flickering like it's taking damage.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.160
And then eventually you get out.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:15.320
And like, yeah, that was like a, wow, really makes you think moment.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:20.920
That, once again, is just kind of nonsense in the context of the rest of the game.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.100
But I did appreciate that design.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:30.160
But the rest of the game remains kind of this, you know, just long-winded, don't get hurt

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:35.820
by any enemies, make it from one end of the stage to the other, and constantly keep riding

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:41.840
these floating devices, whether they are balloons or sheep heads, whatever, constantly keep

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.380
using them to make your way through this maze.

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.880
That is the core crux of the game.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:54.700
The original game, obviously, being developed by Taito, the NES port by Software Creations,

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:55.900
which is a British studio.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:02.060
So that means that we have a Japanese arcade game about New Zealand, converted by a British

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:07.960
team, sold in America under a name that mentions neither Japan nor New Zealand.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:10.420
Remember, we got rid of the New Zealand story part.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:13.500
So now it's just all over the place.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.760
I don't know how to explain this one to anyone.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:23.820
And I don't think calling it Kiwi Craze is, like, harmful in any way either.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:28.800
No, that's really the main point of this episode is to defend the name change.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.980
Right. The name change is perfect.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:31.840
Perfect. Yeah.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:33.360
It's alliterative.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.760
A Kiwi, I think, is a fun word to say.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.420
Really dragging this one out for you.

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.860
Listen, OK, forget about the title, though.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:46.780
Even just the stages themselves, right?

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.060
So again, it's not do not picture Mario.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.600
This is, you know, this is a maze.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:54.160
And so stages are not corridors.

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:56.660
They can scroll in any four directions.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:00.400
You know, the goal is always to find the caged Kiwi.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.940
There is really like nothing else in the way.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:06.120
There isn't like a key you need to find.

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:13.760
There aren't a certain amount of enemies you need to kill before the gate to the cage will open.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:15.200
There's nothing like that.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.660
And the map does not tell you.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:20.740
